# toolchain.list -- the C compiler, ON the machine.
#
# THE MACHINE COMPILES ITS OWN SOURCE.  This is what puts a compiler of OURS on
# an `extended' image, on a project whose stated position is that the native
# compiler is the product and that the deliverable runs on the C900.  The other
# compiler in the tree is lists/toolchain-1985.list, the Mark Williams chain as
# the reference machine shipped with it; that one reproduces a system and this
# one equips the forward port.  os/tests/buildenv, which maps a toolchain in
# from the host, proves the compiler RUNS on the machine; this list is what
# also puts it on the disk.
#
# WHAT IS STAGED is the toolchain repository's COMPILER ENVIRONMENT, `ours' --
# the cc0/cc1/cc2 self-host fixpoint, the driver, the assembler and the linker,
# all Z8001 binaries, with the C library and the headers of this same extended
# tree (see that repository's docs/ENVIRONMENTS.md, and host/build-env.sh which
# composes it).  It is the SAME tree the buildenv test maps in, so the compiler
# an image carries and the compiler that harness proves is one artifact rather
# than two builds of one idea.
#
# `ours' AND NOT `inherited' OR `mwc1985'.  Those two are the COHERENT tree's
# own compiler and Mark Williams' recovered 1985 binaries -- neither is built
# from source in any repository of this project, so neither can discharge a
# -src package (PACKAGE-FORMAT, WHAT `-src' COVERS), and the component that
# ships the 1985 bytes declines the kind out loud instead.  This component does
# not: the toolchain publishes a source map for `ours' alone and removes it for
# the others, so the environment named here and the sources the package answers
# for cannot come apart.
#
# EVERYTHING IS ON /usr, AND THAT IS AN ARITHMETIC RESULT, NOT A PREFERENCE.
# The driver looks its parts up by path and every default path it uses has a
# /usr arm (src/cc/coh/cc.c with host/include/path.h: P_BIN passes on DEFPATH
# ":/bin:/usr/bin", P_LIB passes, crts0.o and libc.a on DEFLIBPATH
# "/lib:/usr/lib", and cpp's DEFDISK is "/usr/include" outright), so `cc x.c'
# works with no options from here.  MEASURED, by packing coherent3-dev and
# coherent3-full-test -- the same media, the same userland, this list the only
# difference -- /usr goes from 27,936 free blocks of 39,671 to 26,249, so this
# list costs 1,687 blocks (about 864 KB) and leaves 66.2% free.  The ROOT
# filesystem of that media has 3,258 blocks free in total: putting the compiler
# there would have taken half of its entire slack and pushed it at or under
# check-image.py's 20% floor, which is a refusal and not a judgement call.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why:
#
#   ar(1)   base.list already ships /bin/ar, built from the userland's own
#           cmd/ar.c.  The environment carries one for a guest that has no
#           system at all; an image that has base does not need a second.
#   cc3     the fourth pass exists in the toolchain build and the environment
#           does not stage it: cc2 emits the object directly here.  A file
#           nothing runs is a file that goes stale unnoticed.
#   the kernel headers.  /usr/include is the USERLAND compile-time interface.
#           Building a kernel or a loadable driver on the machine needs
#           /usr/sys/h and /usr/sys/z8001/h as well, which no build of ours
#           installs on any image yet; a dist that claimed to build a kernel
#           and could not would be the same defect this list is fixing.
#
# NO `package' LINE.  A list with one is an addressable unit of a release
# channel (os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT); this one is not, on purpose: `ours' is the
# toolchain repository's own artifact, built and packaged by that repository,
# the same way packages/include.pkg and packages/libc.pkg already are its
# -dev packages rather than this component's.  The userland stages these
# entries only to compose the extended tree (see the file below); it does not
# cut a toolchain-src or toolchain-man archive over them, so it never carries
# an obligation -- the source map, the licence text -- that belongs to the
# repository that builds `ours'.

# --- the directories ---------------------------------------------------------
# /usr/bin AND /usr/lib ARE NOT HERE.  A `d' entry is NOT idempotent -- dist.py
# refuses two entries staging one path on one filesystem, because the second
# would silently overwrite the first's mode and owner -- and both directories
# are already staged by whichever command set a dist carries (base.list, or
# base.list).  There is no dist without it, and a
# dist that managed it would be refused here by name for the missing parent
# rather than shipping a compiler nothing can reach.
#
# /usr/include and its two subdirectories are filled by the `t' entries at the
# foot of this file, which make a directory as well as filling it.  base.list's
# skeleton declares /usr/include with a `d' -- the 0.7.3 root has the directory
# whether or not a compiler is installed -- and dist.py takes one `d' and one
# `t' at one path as one directory, the `d' declaring it and the `t' filling
# it (dist/FORMAT).  A second `d' here would be a refusal.  No other list
# stages the headers; a compile-time header tree belongs to whoever ships the
# compiler.

# --- the driver, the assembler and the linker --------------------------------
# P_BIN passes: `cc' execs them by name off DEFPATH, so /usr/bin is where they
# are found with no -B and no PATH of the operator's own.
f	/usr/bin/cc	755 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/bin/cc
f	/usr/bin/as	755 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/bin/as
f	/usr/bin/ld	755 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/bin/ld

# --- the compiler passes -----------------------------------------------------
# P_LIB passes: searched on DEFLIBPATH, which is why these are not in /usr/bin
# and are not on anybody's $PATH.  cc0 is the parser, cc1 the optimiser and
# code selector, cc2 the Z8001 emitter.
f	/usr/lib/cc0	755 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/lib/cc0
f	/usr/lib/cc1	755 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/lib/cc1
f	/usr/lib/cc2	755 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/lib/cc2
# pass[CRT].p_pln is the literal "crts0.o": the driver asks the C runtime
# startoff for by that name and no other, so it is installed under it.
f	/usr/lib/crts0.o	644 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/lib/crts0.o
# makelib() composes "lib" + the -l name + ".a", so the default -lc resolves to
# libc.a here.  This is the SAME library the images' own programs are linked
# against on the host -- the environment takes it from this tree.
f	/usr/lib/libc.a	644 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/lib/libc.a
# -lm resolves the same way, to libm.a: the maths library, which cc does not
# link unless it is named.
f	/usr/lib/libm.a	644 0 1	src=host/build/env/ours/lib/libm.a

# --- the debugger ------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERY OTHER ENTRY OF THIS COMPONENT comes out of the toolchain repository's
# compiler environment, host/build/env/ours.  This one comes out of the
# USERLAND build, because that is the tree that builds it, which is a fact
# about where the producer is and not a gap to close by moving a build.
#
# db(1), COHERENT's symbolic debugger: it reads a core file or drives a live
# process through ptrace(2), which is a development tool and so ships with the
# development tools rather than in the minimum system 0.7.3 put it in.  Its
# source is split by MACHINE -- base/cmd/db/trace0.c..trace6.c plus a z8001/
# directory holding the instruction table, the ptrace layer and the stack
# unwinder -- which is why the userland's build-cmd.sh names it specially.
#
# /usr/bin, beside cc/as/ld, for the same two reasons they are there: it is on
# the driver's DEFPATH ":/bin:/usr/bin" so the name resolves with no PATH of
# the operator's own, and the root filesystem's slack is what this component
# must not spend.  lists/toolchain-1985.list stages the recovered 1985
# debugger at /bin/db; that is a different artifact, kept at a different path,
# and no live distribution includes both lists.
f	/usr/bin/db	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/db

# --- the headers -------------------------------------------------------------
# cpp's DEFDISK, so #include <stdio.h> resolves with no -I.
#
# THREE TREE ENTRIES AND NOT ONE, because `t' is FLAT: dist.py stages and packs
# the top-level FILES of a tree and silently ignores its subdirectories (see
# `elif e.type == "t"' in both Dist._stage and component_kind).  One entry over
# usr/include would ship 62 headers of 156 and drop <sys/*.h> entirely -- an
# image that boots, a compiler that runs, and every program that includes
# <sys/types.h> failing.  A subdirectory named here is a subdirectory somebody
# has to notice.
t	/usr/include		src=host/build/env/ours/usr/include
t	/usr/include/sys	src=host/build/env/ours/usr/include/sys
t	/usr/include/netinet	src=host/build/env/ours/usr/include/netinet
# end of toolchain.list
